      "page_idx": 0,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.122,
            0.537,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "Propositions and their Converses"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.716,
            0.068,
            0.866,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.757,
            0.174,
            0.823,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "0905CH09"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.259,
            0.484,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": "Consider the following statement:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.284,
            0.881,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "**Statement 1:** If two sides of a triangle are equal, then the angles opposite the equal sides are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.328,
            0.872,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "Such a statement that is either true or false is called a **proposition**. Now consider the following proposition:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.372,
            0.881,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": "**Statement 2:** If two angles of a triangle are equal, then the sides opposite the equal angles have equal lengths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.416,
            0.882,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "The first proposition is of the form ‘if X then Y’. The second is of the form ‘if Y then X’. We say that the second proposition is the **converse** of the first proposition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.474,
            0.881,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "The statement ‘if X then Y’ is also written as ‘X **implies** Y’. We will use both formulations interchangeably."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.512,
            0.756,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "If a proposition is true, then is its converse always true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.549,
            0.401,
            0.571
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.585,
            0.851,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "We have proved the first statement in an earlier grade. Is the second statement true? Can you prove it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.628,
            0.802,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** Draw the altitude from the vertex containing the third angle.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.665,
            0.842,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider some examples of propositions and their converses,"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.69,
            0.287,
            0.709
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 1:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.708,
            0.679,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": "• Proposition P: If it rains, then the road is wet."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.728,
            0.715,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "• Converse Q: If the road is wet, then it has rained."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.753,
            0.881,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "Proposition P is true but its converse, Q, may not be true. For instance, the road may be wet because a tanker spilled water on the road!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.797,
            0.882,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": "A situation that illustrates why a given proposition is not necessarily true is called a **counterexample**. It refers to an example that contradicts the stated proposition."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
